<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miriam Makeba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/2008">2008</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Zenzile Miriam Makeba, Mama Africa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miriam Makeba, nicknamed Mama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, was a South African singer, songwriter, actress and civil rights activist who campaigned against apartheid. Born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, she sang with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> Brothers and the Skylarks in the 1950s, and a role in the anti-apartheid film Come Back, Africa (1959) took her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where Harry Belafonte became her mentor, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. In 1960 her passport was cancelled when she tried to return for her mother's funeral, and she remained in exile until 1990. She recorded &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pata-pata">Pata Pata</a>&quot; (1967), shared a Grammy with Belafonte and testified against the South African government at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>; she died of a heart attack at a concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba.jpg" alt="Miriam Makeba" width="330" height="580" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reprise Records · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miriam_Makeba_(1968).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Worrod re-recorded it at Equator Sound Studios and marketed it until it became known internationally, and <b>Miriam Makeba</b>'s early recording, which added a third verse, made it famous across Africa and then the world.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malaika">Malaika</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miriam Makeba</b>, who carried many protest songs of the period inside and outside South Africa, made &quot;Meadowlands&quot; widely known, and it became an anthem of the movement against apartheid.</blockquote><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2004 the readers and listeners of the BBC voted it Africa's favourite song, ahead of Brenda Fassie's &quot;Vuli Ndlela&quot;, Fela Kuti's &quot;Lady&quot;, Franco's &quot;Mario&quot; and <b>Miriam Makeba</b>'s version of &quot;Malaika&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sweet-mother">Sweet Mother</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miriam Makeba</b> sang &quot;Mbube&quot; at President John F. Kennedy's birthday in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-lion-sleeps-tonight">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in Castel Volturno, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Johannesburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5290 3126 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miriam Makeba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5740 3396h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5740 3396h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
